# Where a workspace is kept

A workspace is the set of looms that one `.loom/config.yaml` file governs, and Loom assumes it is a folder. The compiler walks that folder to find the looms. The configuration service climbs out of it to find the configuration file. The language server reads files directly.

This chapter gives Loom two storage environments. The file system keeps working as it does today. Remote storage is any service that honours Loom's storage contract at a web address, or URL, and it keeps its data however it likes, in a database, an object store, or a vault. The looms stay the same plain text, the passes stay the same, and a new service, `WorkspaceSource`, hides the difference from the compiler and the language server.

## A loom stays text

A loom is text wherever it is kept. Every position in Loom counts characters into that text, so a diagnostic, a source mapping, and a rename edit all point at offsets in it. Remote storage may hold a loom in any shape, as one record or as many blocks. It must hand Loom the complete text, and the same bytes on every request until the loom changes.

The parsed tree stays derived. The `LoomCorpusAstBuilder` service builds it from the text, and the `LoomDiskCache` service keeps a copy only to save time, so deleting the cache loses nothing. A storage service that wants a tree of sections for its own queries derives one the same way.

## What a source must answer

The `WorkspaceSource` service is how Loom reaches a workspace. It names each loom by a location, a string that identifies the loom and means nothing to the compiler. It answers two questions that may take time, and five that may not.

The first two fetch text. The `open` method hands over a whole `Workspace` value, which holds the settings and the text of every loom. The `read` method hands over one loom's text again after that loom changes. Both may wait on a network, and both fail with the existing `ReadError`.

The other five answer at once. The `corpusOf` method lists the looms that share a loom's corpus, the looms the compiler builds together with it. The `workspaceOf` method names the workspace a loom belongs to, and for a loom outside any workspace it names the loom's own folder. The `placeOf` method says where a sink of a loom lands, the path the compiler records for the file the sink names. The `locationOf` and `uriOf` methods cross between a location and a uniform resource identifier, or URI, the address that the Language Server Protocol uses.

A `Location` is a string, the type the corpus model names a loom by, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" describes.

=>

export interface Workspace {
  readonly location: Location
  readonly manifest: Option.Option<WorkspaceManifest>
  readonly looms: ReadonlyMap<Location, string>
}

export interface WorkspaceSourceApi {
  readonly open: (workspace: Location) => Effect.Effect<Workspace, ReadError>
  readonly read: (loom: Location) => Effect.Effect<string, ReadError>
  readonly corpusOf: (loom: Location) => Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Location>>
  readonly workspaceOf: (loom: Location) => Location
  readonly placeOf: (loom: Location, sink: string) => Effect.Effect<Location>
  readonly locationOf: (uri: URI) => Location
  readonly uriOf: (loom: Location) => URI
}

~

`WorkspaceSource` takes the shape of the `FrameQuery` and `Composition` capabilities in Part VII's chapter "The service contract". Its own layer dies, because only an environment's layer can say where looms are kept.

=>

export class WorkspaceSource extends Context.Service<WorkspaceSource>()('WorkspaceSource', {
  make: Effect.die('WorkspaceSource must be provided by an environment') as Effect.Effect<WorkspaceSourceApi>,
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

~

Loom ships one source for each environment. The `FileSystemSource` layer reads folders the way Loom does today. The `RemoteSource` layer speaks the storage contract. Both provide the `WorkspaceSource` service, and a test can supply its own through the `Layer.succeed` function.

## Folders as a source

The `FileSystemSource` layer keeps a workspace in a folder on a disk. A location is an absolute path there, so every loom in a folder keeps the name it has today. A read is the synchronous file read of the `fromDisk` function, so it never waits. It fails with a `ReadError` when the file cannot be read.

=>

export const fromDisk = (path: Path): Effect.Effect<string, ReadError> =>
  Effect.try({
    try: () => readFileSync(path, 'utf8'),
    catch: (cause) => new ReadError({ path, cause }),
  })

~

The `open` method takes the workspace's folder. It reads the folder's `.loom/config.yaml` file through the `LoomConfig` service, then walks the corpus folder the manifest names, `corpus` unless the manifest names another. The `loomsIn` function, which the chapter "The looms in a project" describes, does the walk under the project's rules. So `open` hands over the same looms that the compiler builds and the tangler writes. A folder that holds no configuration opens as a loose workspace, with no manifest and every loom under the folder.

=>

const corpusFolderOf = (workspace: Location, manifest: Option.Option<WorkspaceManifest>): Location =>
  Option.match(manifest, {
    onNone: () => workspace,
    onSome: (m) => join(workspace, m.corpus ?? defaultCorpus),
  })

~

The `corpusOf` method finds the looms the compiler builds together with a loom. The configuration the `LoomConfig` service resolves for the loom gives its project and its corpus folder, the nearest folder above it with the corpus folder's name. A loom its project holds shares that corpus folder, or its own folder when no corpus folder lies above it, walked under the project's rules. A loom its project leaves out, such as a test fixture, or a loom outside every workspace, is loose. It shares its own folder with every loom under it, walked under no project's rules, so a fixture that imports from a sibling fixture still finds it.

=>

const corpusWalk = (resolved: ResolvedConfig, loom: Location): ReadonlyArray<Location> =>
  Option.match(
    Option.filter(projectIn(resolved), (project) => holds(project, loom)),
    {
      onNone: () => loomsIn(dirname(loom), Option.none()),
      onSome: (project) =>
        loomsIn(
          Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(resolved.corpusDir), () => dirname(loom)),
          Option.some(project),
        ),
    },
  )

~

A sink lands under the package root, the folder above the nearest corpus folder, or beside its loom when the loom sits in no corpus folder. The `outputPath` function resolves a sink against the package root it is given, or against the loom's own folder when it is given none. The `placeOf` method resolves a sink through it, against the package root that the `LoomConfig` service resolves for the loom. The formatter, which works only on the file system, resolves a sink through it too.

=>

export const outputPath = (packageRoot: Option.Option<string>, entry: Path, sink: string): Path =>
  Option.match(packageRoot, {
    onNone: () => resolvePath(dirname(entry), sink),
    onSome: (root) => resolvePath(root, sink),
  })

~

The `locationOf` method takes a file URI's path, and keeps any other URI whole as its location. So `memory:/a.loom` never collapses into the path `/a.loom`, as the `fsPath` property would make it. The `uriOf` method turns an absolute path back into a file URI, and parses any other location as the URI it was.

=>

const folderOf = (location: Path): Path =>
  statSync(location, { throwIfNoEntry: false })?.isDirectory() === true
    ? location
    : dirname(location)

const locationOfUri = (uri: URI): Location => (uri.scheme === 'file' ? uri.fsPath : uri.toString())

const uriOfLocation = (loom: Location): URI => (isAbsolute(loom) ? URI.file(loom) : URI.parse(loom))

~

The layer gathers the seven answers. The `workspaceOf` method climbs from a loom's folder, or from a folder it is handed, through the `findWorkspace` function, the climb the configuration makes. It names that folder itself when no folder above holds a configuration. `folderOf` tells a folder from a loom on the disk. The layer provides the `LoomConfig` service it reads, as every Loom layer provides its own.

=>

export const FileSystemSource = Layer.effect(
  WorkspaceSource,
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const config = yield* LoomConfig
    const open = (workspace: Location): Effect.Effect<Workspace, ReadError> =>
      Effect.gen(function* () {
        const manifest = Option.fromNullishOr(yield* config.manifest(workspace))
        const resolved = yield* config.resolve(workspace)
        const paths = yield* Effect.sync(() =>
          loomsIn(corpusFolderOf(workspace, manifest), projectIn(resolved)),
        )
        const looms = yield* Effect.forEach(paths, (loom) =>
          fromDisk(loom).pipe(Effect.map((text) => [loom, text] as const)),
        )
        return { location: workspace, manifest, looms: new Map(looms) }
      })
    const source: WorkspaceSourceApi = {
      open,
      read: fromDisk,
      corpusOf: (loom) =>
        config
          .resolve(loom)
          .pipe(Effect.flatMap((resolved) => Effect.sync(() => corpusWalk(resolved, loom)))),
      workspaceOf: (location) =>
        Option.getOrElse(
          Option.fromNullishOr(findWorkspace(folderOf(location))),
          () => folderOf(location),
        ),
      placeOf: (loom, sink) =>
        config
          .resolve(loom)
          .pipe(
            Effect.map(({ packageRoot }) =>
              outputPath(Option.fromNullishOr(packageRoot), loom, sink),
            ),
          ),
      locationOf: locationOfUri,
      uriOf: uriOfLocation,
    }
    return source
  }),
).pipe(Layer.provide(LoomConfig.layer))

~

## Why the text waits in memory

The language server cannot wait for a network. Volar calls the `createVirtualCode` hook synchronously, and the `LoomLanguagePlugin` module answers that call with the `runtime.runSync` method. It answers diagnostics, go-to-definition, and rename the same way. The `runSync` method fails on any effect that has to wait, so a network read cannot run inside those calls.

The text must therefore be in memory before the editor asks for it. The server calls the `open` method while it handles the `initialize` request, which Volar lets it await, and the `DocumentSource` service holds what comes back. Its `hold` method keeps every loom's text, as the chapter "The compile layer" describes. The compiler keeps reading through the `DocumentSource` service as it does today, and each read of a held loom becomes a lookup in memory.

In short, the `WorkspaceSource` service fetches and the `DocumentSource` service remembers.

Holding the text costs almost nothing, because Loom already holds it. Each `LoomModule` value carries its loom's text, and the `warmCorpus` function builds the whole corpus when the server starts. The `tangle` verb reads the whole corpus too, on every run.

An editor can also open a loom from a workspace the server has not opened yet. The file-system source handles this during a synchronous call, because its reads never wait. The remote source never meets the case, because its URL names one workspace.

## The storage contract

Remote storage answers two requests, because only the `open` and `read` methods fetch anything. The other five methods must answer at once, so they cannot cross a network, and the `RemoteSource` layer answers them by rule.

A URL names one workspace, and each loom's location is a URL beneath it, ending in `.loom`. Volar's language lookup and the `isLoom` function both read that ending. Every request carries the secret key in an `Authorization: Bearer` header.

A `GET` request on the workspace's URL returns the workspace as JSON. It holds the contract's version, the workspace's manifest, and every loom's text keyed by location.

```
{
  "contract": 1,
  "manifest": { "corpus": "corpus", "primary": "TypeScript" },
  "looms": {
    "https://storage.example/books/42/corpus/book.loom": "---\nPart: ..."
  }
}
```

A `GET` request on a loom's location returns that loom's text.

An Effect Schema, `WorkspaceJson`, defines the JSON shape, and a service written in any language can honour it. The manifest has the shape `.loom/config.yaml` has on a disk, and a workspace without one leaves it out. New versions of the contract only add fields, so an older Loom still reads a newer service.

=>

export const WorkspaceJson = Schema.Struct({
  contract: Schema.Number,
  manifest: Schema.optional(WorkspaceManifestSchema),
  looms: Schema.Record(Schema.String, Schema.String),
})

~

`fetchText` makes one `GET` request through Effect's `HttpClient` service, with the key as a bearer token, and keeps only an answer whose status is a success. Any failure becomes a `ReadError` that names the URL and the reason, and never the key, which stays in the request's header. `workspaceFrom` decodes a workspace's JSON against `WorkspaceJson` and turns it into the `Workspace` value the `open` method hands over.

=>

const fetchText = (
  client: HttpClient.HttpClient,
  key: Redacted.Redacted<string>,
  url: string,
): Effect.Effect<string, ReadError> =>
  HttpClient.filterStatusOk(client)
    .execute(HttpClientRequest.get(url).pipe(HttpClientRequest.bearerToken(key)))
    .pipe(
      Effect.flatMap((response) => response.text),
      Effect.mapError((cause) => new ReadError({ path: url, cause })),
    )

const workspaceFrom = (url: string, text: string): Effect.Effect<Workspace, ReadError> =>
  Effect.try({
    try: () => JSON.parse(text) as unknown,
    catch: (cause) => new ReadError({ path: url, cause }),
  }).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((json) =>
      Schema.decodeUnknownEffect(WorkspaceJson)(json).pipe(
        Effect.mapError((cause) => new ReadError({ path: url, cause })),
      ),
    ),
    Effect.map((decoded) => ({
      location: url,
      manifest: Option.fromNullishOr(decoded.manifest),
      looms: new Map(Object.entries(decoded.looms)),
    })),
  )

~

The contract holds no write request. The server never saves a loom, so writing belongs to whoever edits the text. Permissions, conflicts, and history belong there too.

The `RemoteSource` layer answers the other five methods without a request. One URL names one workspace, and one workspace holds one corpus, so the `corpusOf` method lists every loom of the workspace the `open` method fetched last. The `workspaceOf` method always names that URL. The `placeOf` method returns the sink unchanged, so a sink stays relative to the workspace. The `locationOf` and `uriOf` methods pass a URI through as a string and parse it back. The layer provides the `HttpClient` service it fetches through, Effect's own client over the platform's `fetch`.

=>

export const RemoteSource = (address: StorageAddress): Layer.Layer<WorkspaceSource> =>
  Layer.effect(
    WorkspaceSource,
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const client = yield* HttpClient.HttpClient
      const listed = yield* Ref.make<ReadonlyArray<Location>>([])
      const read = (loom: Location): Effect.Effect<string, ReadError> =>
        fetchText(client, address.key, loom)
      const source: WorkspaceSourceApi = {
        open: (workspace) =>
          read(workspace).pipe(
            Effect.flatMap((text) => workspaceFrom(workspace, text)),
            Effect.tap((opened) => Ref.set(listed, Array.fromIterable(opened.looms.keys()))),
          ),
        read,
        corpusOf: () => Ref.get(listed),
        workspaceOf: () => address.url,
        placeOf: (_loom, sink) => Effect.succeed(sink),
        locationOf: (uri) => uri.toString(),
        uriOf: (loom) => URI.parse(loom),
      }
      return source
    }),
  ).pipe(Layer.provide(FetchHttpClient.layer))

~

The `RemoteSettings` layer gives the settings that go with it, and `RemoteEnvironment` puts the source and the settings beside a build cache that keeps nothing. Its `manifest` method opens the workspace through the source and keeps the manifest that arrives. Its `resolve` method answers every loom from that kept manifest through the `settingsOfManifest` function of the chapter "Build settings for a file", because the compiler asks for settings inside Volar's synchronous hooks. The server asks for the manifest while it starts, before the compiler asks for any settings.

=>

export const RemoteSettings = Layer.effect(
  WorkspaceSettings,
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const source = yield* WorkspaceSource
    const kept = yield* Ref.make(Option.none<WorkspaceManifest>())
    const settings: WorkspaceSettingsApi = {
      resolve: () => Ref.get(kept).pipe(Effect.map(settingsOfManifest)),
      manifest: (workspace) =>
        source.open(workspace).pipe(
          Effect.map((opened) => opened.manifest),
          Effect.tap((manifest) => Ref.set(kept, manifest)),
        ),
    }
    return settings
  }),
)

export const RemoteEnvironment = (
  address: StorageAddress,
): Layer.Layer<WorkspaceSource | WorkspaceSettings | LoomDiskCache> =>
  Layer.mergeAll(
    RemoteSettings.pipe(Layer.provideMerge(RemoteSource(address))),
    LoomDiskCache.none,
  )

~

## The URL and the key

Two environment variables connect Loom to remote storage. The `LOOM_STORAGE_URL` variable names the workspace, and the `LOOM_STORAGE_KEY` variable holds the secret key. When the first is set, Loom uses the `RemoteSource` layer, and the second must be set too. When the first is unset, Loom uses the `FileSystemSource` layer.

Neither value belongs in the `.loom/config.yaml` file. On the file system that file sits in a repository. In remote storage, Loom needs the key to fetch that file in the first place.

The key stays in an Effect `Redacted` value, so a log line cannot print it. A `StorageAddress` holds the URL and the key together. A wrong key fails the `open` method, so the editor hears of it as it connects.

=>

export interface StorageAddress {
  readonly url: string
  readonly key: Redacted.Redacted<string>
}

~

The two variables are all the language server needs, so it can run on an author's machine or next to the storage.

## A location is a name, not a path

The compiler compares locations and keys its maps by them, and it never takes one apart. Only the source knows what a location means, because the source makes it. The corpus model's `Location` type says so.

The file-system source keeps today's absolute paths as locations, so nothing changes for a loom in a folder.

The `locationOf` and `uriOf` methods replace the uses of `uri.fsPath`, `URI.file`, and `fileURLToPath` in the `LoomLanguagePlugin`, `LoomServer`, and `LoomLanguage` modules. This removes a trap as well. The `fsPath` property ignores a URI's scheme, so `memory:/a.loom` and `file:///a.loom` both come out as `/a.loom`. The `LoomLanguage` service reaches the two methods through the `FrameQuery` capability, because its package cannot depend on the compiler's. Two checks in the server keep reading `fsPath`, each behind a test of the URI's `file` scheme, because each is a rule about folders: the lookup of a nested workspace, and the modified time the `savedLooms` function reads.

The compiler, the corpus model, and every service that keys or compares looms name a loom by a `Location`. Code that works only on the file system and takes a path apart keeps the `Path` type of the chapter "The looms in a project": the walk of the `loomsIn` function, the tangler, the formatter's listing of a folder, the weaver's output folder, and this source's own reads and placement of sinks.

One form of anchor still reads inside a location. The anchor `::[Name](path.loom)` goes through the `resolveRelative` function, which splits the location on `/`. A URL splits the same way, so this anchor keeps working until the chapter "Names through the book" retires it.

## Four folder rules move out

Four rules about folders sit in the compiler and its neighbours. Each one moves into the file-system source or behind a layer, so the compiler no longer depends on a folder.

**Which looms belong.** The compiler's `listFrom` function found the corpus folder, then used the `holds` and `loomsIn` functions to walk it under the rules in `.gitignore` files. The chapter "The looms in a project" describes that walk, and none of it means anything without folders. The walk is now the file-system source's `corpusOf` method. The `list` method of the `Source` interface takes only a loom's path, and the `DocumentSource` service answers it by asking the `corpusOf` method. A remote source lists every loom of its workspace, since one URL names one corpus.

**Where a workspace begins.** The `LoomConfig` service climbs folders to find the `.loom/config.yaml` file, then works out the workspace root, the corpus folder, and the package root from folder names. The `BuildSettings` interface now keeps what a manifest says, the anchor delimiters, the primary language, the variables, and the width, beside the package root that the placement of a sink still reads. The parts of Loom that keep their folders read the other folder facts from `LoomConfig` themselves: the file-system source, the build cache on disk, the tangler, and the formatter. A remote source has no folders to climb, so the settings it gives come from the manifest its `open` method fetched.

**Where a sink lands.** The `outputPath` function resolves a sink against the parent of the corpus folder, or against the loom's own folder when the loom sits outside any corpus folder. So a loom's folder can decide where its output goes. The `outputPath` function now lives in the file-system source, behind its `placeOf` method. The compiler asks `placeOf` for each sink's file, stores the answer in each `TangledFile` and `ComposedFile` value, and never reads it.

**What the cache may keep.** The `LoomDiskCache` service writes to the `.loom/cache` folder, and it checks the text the `DocumentSource` service holds, so it never keeps an unsaved edit. The `LoomCompiler` layer no longer provides the cache. The environment does, so remote storage runs with a cache that keeps nothing, and a server beside the storage builds the corpus again at each start.

## How the server hears of a change

The server needs no new signal. Editors already report changes through the protocol's `workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles` notification, and the `LoomServer` module already reacts to it. The notification carries a URI of any scheme, so an editor working over remote storage sends the same message.

The handler changes in one way. It calls the `read` method for each changed loom, updates the `DocumentSource` service, and then invalidates as it does today. That read may wait, so this work moves out of the `runSync` call and into the fiber the handler already forks to reproject open looms.

The server recognises a change to the settings by the ending of its URI, `/.loom/config.yaml`, and then calls the `open` method again.

The server never saves a loom. Rename and formatting return edits, and the editor applies them and saves. An editor working over remote storage saves through its own service, then sends the notification.

## What keeps its folder

Three things stay on disk.

**What the command line writes.** The `tangle` and `weave` commands only read looms, so they read from either environment and write their output to a folder. The `tangle` verb returns `TangledFile` values and writes nothing, so a tangle of a remote workspace can write its product to the current folder, as "What to work out" notes. The lock stays beside that output, because it records what the tangle wrote. The `format`, `toc`, and `set` commands rewrite a workspace's own text, so they work only on the file system.

**The programs Loom runs.** The `.loom/services` folder and the `~/.loom/typescript` folder hold language services and TypeScript 7. Neither holds a workspace's content, so both stay on the machine that runs the server. The `LOOM_HOME` variable already moves the services out of the workspace folder.

**Language services that need a folder.** TypeScript 7 runs as `tsc --lsp` in a real folder and reads `tsconfig.json` and `node_modules` from it. A workspace with no folder gets every feature Loom answers itself: its own diagnostics, go-to-definition, find-references, rename, semantic tokens, composition, and weaving. It gets no type checking from TypeScript until something gives that service a folder. That limit belongs to the language service, not to the compiler.

## Choosing the environment at startup

An environment answers three questions for Loom: where the looms are kept, how the workspace is configured, and what a build may keep between processes. The `WorkspaceSource` service answers the first, the `WorkspaceSettings` service of the chapter "Build settings for a file" answers the second, and the `LoomDiskCache` service of the chapter "The build cache on disk" answers the third. A workspace keeps one configuration, its `.loom/config.yaml` file, and the environment decides how Loom reads it.

The compiler asks `WorkspaceSettings` for a loom's build settings. The language plugin's `loomServicePlugins` function asks it for the manifest that names the languages a workspace activates. The server's `openWorkspace` function asks it whether a folder holds a configuration at all. Only the parts of Loom that work on the file system alone read `LoomConfig` themselves: the file-system source and settings, the build cache on disk, the tangler, and the formatter.

Each environment provides the three services together. `FileSystemEnvironment` is the file system's: the `FileSystemSource` layer, the `FileSystemSettings` layer, which reads `.loom/config.yaml` through `LoomConfig`, and the `LoomDiskCache` layer, which keeps built modules in the workspace's `.loom/cache` folder. The server's runtime and the `loom` program both build on it. `RemoteEnvironment` is remote storage's. It puts the `RemoteSource` layer beside settings that read the manifest its workspace's JSON carries, and beside the `LoomDiskCache.none` layer, which keeps nothing.

=>

export const FileSystemEnvironment = Layer.mergeAll(
  FileSystemSource,
  FileSystemSettings,
  LoomDiskCache.layer,
)

~

The server's runtime stays a module constant, because Volar's synchronous hooks reach it from all over the module. So the environment is chosen inside the layer the runtime is built over, rather than handed to the `startLanguageServer` function. `Environment` is that layer. It reads the `LOOM_STORAGE_URL` variable through Effect's `Config` module. When the variable is set, it reads the key from `LOOM_STORAGE_KEY` and builds `RemoteEnvironment` over the two. When it is unset, it builds `FileSystemEnvironment`. A URL set without a key fails the layer, and the server reports the failure as the editor connects.

=>

export const Environment = Layer.unwrap(
  Config.option(Config.String('LOOM_STORAGE_URL')).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(
      Option.match({
        onNone: () => Effect.succeed(FileSystemEnvironment),
        onSome: (url) =>
          Config.Redacted('LOOM_STORAGE_KEY').pipe(
            Effect.map((key) => RemoteEnvironment({ url, key })),
          ),
      }),
    ),
  ),
)

~

The `loom` program still builds on `FileSystemEnvironment` alone. Its `tangle` command finds the lock and the workspace through the folder it is handed, so it reads remote storage only once it learns to write a remote workspace's product to a folder of its own, as "What to work out" notes.

## A workspace with no folder

Two tests decide when this chapter is done. Both run a small storage service on a local port, which holds its looms in a map behind a key. That service is also the stub a consumer of the contract starts from.

The first test starts the language server with that service's URL and key, and with no folder. It opens a loom and receives diagnostics, follows a definition into another loom, and renames a section across looms. It then sends a change notification and watches the diagnostics move. Every read crosses a real socket and really waits, so any code still reading inside a synchronous call fails this test.

The second test starts the server with a wrong key and expects the failure during the `initialize` request, with no key in the message.

The existing tests keep passing, with their stubs updated to the new shapes. The `loom tangle` command writes the same bytes over this book.

## Testing the file-system source

The cases build a workspace in a temporary folder: a `.loom/config.yaml` file, a book at the root of the corpus folder, and a chapter in a part's folder beneath it. A loose folder beside the workspace holds one loom and no configuration.

Opening the workspace hands over its manifest and both looms with their text. A loom names its workspace, and the loose loom names its own folder. A chapter shares its corpus with the book, and the loose loom's corpus holds the loose loom alone. A sink of the book lands in the workspace's folder, which is the package root, and a sink of the loose loom lands beside it. A file URI crosses to its path and back, and a `memory:` URI keeps its scheme both ways.

`DocumentSource` answers a held loom from memory. The last case holds the opened workspace's looms and rewrites the book on the disk. A read of the book still returns the text that `DocumentSource` held, and a read of a loom it never held returns the text on the disk. Once `DocumentSource` forgets the book, a read of the book returns the rewritten text on the disk.

=>

import { describe, expect, it } from 'bun:test'
import { Effect, Layer, ManagedRuntime, Option } from 'effect'
import { mkdirSync, mkdtempSync, realpathSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os'
import { join } from 'node:path'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import { DocumentSource } from '../src/LoomCompiler'
import { FileSystemSource, WorkspaceSource } from '../src/WorkspaceSource'

const runtime = ManagedRuntime.make(DocumentSource.layer.pipe(Layer.provideMerge(FileSystemSource)))

const source = () => runtime.runPromise(WorkspaceSource.pipe(Effect.map((s) => s)))

const folder = (prefix: string): string => realpathSync(mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), prefix)))

const workspace = () => {
  const root = folder('loom-workspace-')
  mkdirSync(join(root, '.loom'))
  mkdirSync(join(root, 'corpus', 'part'), { recursive: true })
  writeFileSync(join(root, '.loom', 'config.yaml'), 'corpus: corpus\n')
  const book = join(root, 'corpus', 'book.loom')
  const chapter = join(root, 'corpus', 'part', 'chapter.loom')
  writeFileSync(book, '# Book\n')
  writeFileSync(chapter, '# Chapter\n')
  return { root, book, chapter }
}

const looseLoom = () => {
  const root = folder('loom-loose-')
  const loom = join(root, 'loose.loom')
  writeFileSync(loom, '# Loose\n')
  return { root, loom }
}

describe('FileSystemSource — a workspace kept in a folder', () => {
  it('opens a workspace with its manifest and the text of every loom', async () => {
    const { root, book, chapter } = workspace()
    const opened = await runtime.runPromise(WorkspaceSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((s) => s.open(root))))
    expect(opened.location).toBe(root)
    expect(Option.isSome(opened.manifest)).toBe(true)
    expect([...opened.looms.keys()].sort()).toEqual([book, chapter].sort())
    expect(opened.looms.get(chapter)).toBe('# Chapter\n')
  })

  it("names a loom's workspace, or its own folder when it has none", async () => {
    const { root, chapter } = workspace()
    const loose = looseLoom()
    const s = await source()
    expect(s.workspaceOf(chapter)).toBe(root)
    expect(s.workspaceOf(loose.loom)).toBe(loose.root)
  })

  it("lists a loom's corpus, or a loose loom's folder", async () => {
    const { book, chapter } = workspace()
    const loose = looseLoom()
    const s = await source()
    const corpus = (loom: string) => runtime.runPromise(s.corpusOf(loom))
    expect([...(await corpus(chapter))].sort()).toEqual([book, chapter].sort())
    expect(await corpus(loose.loom)).toEqual([loose.loom])
  })

  it('places a sink under the package root, or beside a loose loom', async () => {
    const { root, book } = workspace()
    const loose = looseLoom()
    const s = await source()
    const place = (loom: string, sink: string) => runtime.runPromise(s.placeOf(loom, sink))
    expect(await place(book, 'src/book.ts')).toBe(join(root, 'src', 'book.ts'))
    expect(await place(loose.loom, 'loose.ts')).toBe(join(loose.root, 'loose.ts'))
  })

  it('crosses between locations and URIs without losing a scheme', async () => {
    const { book } = workspace()
    const s = await source()
    expect(s.locationOf(URI.file(book))).toBe(book)
    expect(s.uriOf(book).toString()).toBe(URI.file(book).toString())
    expect(s.locationOf(URI.parse('memory:/a.loom'))).toBe('memory:/a.loom')
    expect(s.uriOf('memory:/a.loom').scheme).toBe('memory')
  })

  it('lets DocumentSource answer a held loom from memory and any other from disk', async () => {
    const { root, book } = workspace()
    const loose = looseLoom()
    const read = (path: string) =>
      runtime.runPromise(DocumentSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((d) => d.read(path))))
    const opened = await runtime.runPromise(WorkspaceSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((s) => s.open(root))))
    await runtime.runPromise(DocumentSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((d) => d.hold(opened.looms))))
    writeFileSync(book, '# Rewritten\n')
    expect(await read(book)).toBe('# Book\n')
    expect(await read(loose.loom)).toBe('# Loose\n')
    await runtime.runPromise(DocumentSource.pipe(Effect.flatMap((d) => d.forget(book))))
    expect(await read(book)).toBe('# Rewritten\n')
  })
})

~

## What the source draws on

Effect supplies the service, the layer, `Option`, and the effect type. Node's `fs` reads a file, and its `path` takes a location apart, joins a corpus folder to its workspace, and resolves a sink. The `vscode-uri` package parses and builds the URIs the editor speaks. From `@athrio/loom-config` come the `LoomConfig` service, the `ResolvedConfig` it resolves for a loom, the `WorkspaceManifest` type, and the `findWorkspace` and `defaultCorpus` rules this source shares with the configuration. `Location` names a loom, `Path` a file on the disk, and `ReadError` a read that failed. `FileSystemSettings`, from the chapter "Build settings for a file", and `LoomDiskCache`, from the chapter "The build cache on disk", join the sources in each environment. `projectIn` finds a loom's project, `holds` asks whether the project holds it, and `loomsIn` walks its corpus.

=>

